    14	# Design analysis for the combo variant
    15	# a) Concrete realization and behavior trace:
    16	#    The original task taught a rule with a local colored pair plus a nearby teal stencil, then applied that
    17	#    one-step rewrite independently to scattered source pixels. Here the instruction area becomes a chain of
    18	#    framed training pairs. Each legend row has two 5x5 teal hollow frames with 3x3 interiors:
    19	#    the left interior is a monochrome source sprite, and the right interior is the corresponding monochrome
    20	#    target sprite. Consecutive legends are linked because rule i's right sprite is exactly rule i+1's left
    21	#    sprite, including both shape and color. A concrete trace is:
    22	#    - Original: isolated red pixel + nearby teal stencil example means "red dot -> blue copied stencil".
    23	#    - Combo step 1: framed example shows [red dot] -> [blue L].
    24	#    - Combo step 2: next framed example shows [blue L] -> [yellow T].
    25	#    - Application: each scattered red dot becomes a blue L after step 1, then that blue L becomes a yellow T
    26	#      after step 2. The output therefore contains only the final yellow T sprites, not the intermediate blue L.
    27	#    The main difference is that the rule must be learned from analogy panels and then composed across steps.
    28	# b) Complexity calibration:
    29	#    The chain length is 2-3, which is large enough that the second step materially matters but still small
    30	#    enough for humans to track. Every sprite lives inside a 3x3 board and is monochrome, so the rule space is
    31	#    visually structured rather than arbitrary. The first stage is always a single-pixel source, preserving a
    32	#    recognizable link to the original task, while later stages use nontrivial connected sprites so the chain is
    33	#    visibly different from a one-step recolor-and-stamp puzzle.
    34	# c) Implicit assumption audit against the original main():
    35	#    The old solver depended on three assumptions that no longer hold:
    36	#    - A rule is not encoded by an exposed adjacent source-target color pair anymore.
    37	#    - Teal no longer marks the stencil itself; teal now only marks hollow instruction frames.
    38	#    - Multiple rules are not parallel color mappings; they must be ordered and executed sequentially.
    39	#    The new solver therefore assumes:
    40	#    - teal appears only as 5x5 hollow frames around 3x3 training boards;
    41	#    - each left/right board is monochrome and contains the center cell, which is the shared anchor;
    42	#    - the rule order is discoverable from exact stage continuity: previous output signature == next input
